<article class="env-remark" id="0BP8">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BP8">Remark <span data-tag="0BP8">15.92.20</span>.</a> In <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/Beauville-Laszlo">Beauville-Laszlo</a>]</span> it is assumed that $f$ is a nonzerodivisor in $R$ and $R' = R^\wedge $, which gives a glueing pair by Lemma <a href="/tag/0BNR" data-tag="0BNR">15.92.6</a>. Even in this setting Theorem <a href="/tag/0BP2" data-tag="0BP2">15.92.16</a> says something new: the results of <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/Beauville-Laszlo">Beauville-Laszlo</a>]</span> only apply to modules on which $f$ is a nonzerodivisor (and hence glueable in our sense, see Lemma <a href="/tag/0BNW" data-tag="0BNW">15.92.10</a>). Lemma <a href="/tag/0BP6" data-tag="0BP6">15.92.19</a> also provides a slight extension of the results of <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/Beauville-Laszlo">Beauville-Laszlo</a>]</span>: not only can we allow $M$ to have nonzero $f$-power torsion, we do not even require it to be glueable. </p>
